WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.040
Cloud security becomes practical when it is treated as an operating routine.

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:09.620
The routine starts with knowing the environment. Identify assets, owners, data types,

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.960
responsibility boundaries, and business purpose.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:16.540
Know which account, subscription, project,

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:21.320
or tenant a resource belongs to. Know whether it is production, development,

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.260
shared infrastructure, or temporary work.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.920
Security decisions are much stronger when the team knows what it is protecting.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:28.940
Next,

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:33.880
understand the shared responsibility boundary. The provider secures important parts of the

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:35.620
underlying platform,

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:41.360
but the customer still controls configuration, identity, data access, network exposure,

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:43.900
monitoring, and response.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:49.080
Do not let shared responsibility become shared uncertainty. Assign owners for access,

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:54.260
logging, data, workload configuration, exceptions, and remediation.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:59.840
Control identity and access carefully. Cloud control often happens through identities,

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:05.580
roles, policies, service accounts, workload identities, API keys,

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:07.640
and temporary credentials.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:12.260
Use least privilege, multi-factor authentication for privileged access,

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:17.040
short-lived credentials where possible, secret management, and regular access review.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:23.020
Permissions should match job needs and change as systems change. Limit network exposure.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:28.440
Ask what is reachable from the internet, what can talk internally, which ports are open,

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:30.820
and which services should be private.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:36.320
Public exposure should be intentional, minimal, protected, and monitored. Segmentation,

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:40.060
firewall rules, security groups, private endpoints,

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.840
and routing should match the intended architecture.

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:48.180
Protect data and workloads. Know where data lives, who owns it, who can access it,

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.160
how it is encrypted, how keys are managed, where backups exist,

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:54.620
and when data should be retained or deleted.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:02:00.360
For workloads, maintain images and templates, patch dependencies, harden configuration,

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:04.980
limit runtime identity, protect secrets, and monitor runtime behavior.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:10.720
Finally, keep watching and improving. Enable, retain, and protect logs.

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:16.460
Review identity activity, configuration changes, public exposure, data access,

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:19.560
disabled logging, and risky resource creation.

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:24.180
Use governance to make secure behavior repeatable through structure, tags, ownership,

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:30.000
baselines, infrastructure as code, drift detection, and clear exception handling.

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:34.060
The key message is simple: cloud security is not just a checklist.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:40.360
It is a repeatable way of operating cloud systems safely. Know the asset, limit access,

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:46.180
protect data, monitor activity, review configuration, and improve the baseline over time.
